%N A386569 The number of binary self numbers not exceeding 2^n.
%C A386569 Let F(t) be the number of nonnegative integers v such that t = v + s(v), where s(v) is the binary digit sum of v. Thus t is a binary self (Colombian) number iff F(t) = 0. By Theorem 7 of Alekseyev and Sloane, if t = 2^m + 1 + k is the canonical representation, then F(t) = F(k) + F(m-k-2), with F(r) = 0 for r < 0. It allows efficient computing of a(n) for large n. - _Max Alekseyev_, Sep 17 2026
%H A386569 Max Alekseyev, <a href="/A386569/b386569.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..1000</a>
%H A386569 M. A. Alekseyev and N. J. A. Sloane, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.14365">On Kaprekar's Junction Numbers</a>, Journal of Combinatorics and Number Theory 12:3 (2022), 115-155; arXiv:2112.14365 [math.NT], 2021-2022.
%F A386569 Limit_{n->oo} a(n)/2^n = A242403.
%e A386569 There are 3 binary self numbers that do no exceed 2^3 = 8: 1, 4 and 6. Hence a(3) = 3.
%t A386569 selfQ[n_] := AllTrue[Range[n, n - Floor@Log2[n], -1], # + DigitCount[#, 2, 1] != n &]; a[n_] := Count[Range[2^n], _?selfQ]; Array[a, 16, 0]
%Y A386569 Cf. A010061, A242403, A337078, A382452, A386568.
